Vulnerability: Broken Authentication / 2FA Bypass
Platform: PortSwigger Web Security Academy
Objective
Bypass the second authentication factor by manipulating the application's authentication flow and accessing the victim's account without completing 2FA.
Endpoints
/login
/login2
/my-account
/login — accepts the username and password.
/login2 — handles the 4-digit 2FA verification.
/my-account — authenticated account dashboard.
Methodology
I first submitted the primary credentials through /login.
An initial attempt to retrieve the 2FA code before submitting valid credentials resulted in an empty inbox. This showed that the verification code was generated only after successful first-factor authentication.
Instead of attempting to brute-force the 4-digit code, I investigated whether the application properly enforced the authentication workflow.
After successfully authenticating with the supplied credentials, I was redirected to the 2FA verification stage at /login2.
I then manually modified the browser URL and requested:
GET /my-account?id=carlos
The application accepted the partially authenticated session and allowed access to Carlos's account without requiring completion of the 2FA step.
The account page displayed:
Your username is carlos
This confirmed that the second authentication factor had effectively been bypassed.
Why the Vulnerability Exists
The application trusted the partially authenticated session without verifying that the 2FA step had been successfully completed before granting access to the protected /my-account endpoint.
The security issue was therefore not the strength of the 2FA code. The flaw was in the application's authentication-state enforcement.
Key Takeaways
2FA must be enforced server-side before protected resources are accessible.
A valid first factor should not automatically grant access to authenticated functionality.
Authentication testing should examine the entire workflow, not just individual login forms.
Directly requesting protected endpoints can reveal broken authentication controls.
Not every 2FA vulnerability requires guessing or brute-forcing the OTP.
